  "response": "열한 해 동안 출근했던 곳이 곧 사라질 수 있다는 것 자체가 큰 변화인데, 그보다 더 무섭다고 느끼는 게 '아무도 안 말하고 지내는 하루'라는 거죠. 직장이라는 게 단순히 일하는 자리가 아니라 매일 얼굴을 보고 말을 주고받는 관계가 자연스럽게 만들어지는 곳이었을 텐데, 그 통째로 끊길 수 있다는 게 훨씬 더 현실적으로 무섭게 느껴지셨을 것 같아요.\n\n그 무서움이라는 게, 막상 일이 끝나고 나면 실제로 그렇게 될 것 같은 예감에서 오는 건가요, 아니면 지금도 이미 조금씩 그런 느낌이 들고 있는 건가요?",
  "reasoning": "The user is a counselor responding to a client. Let me analyze what the client has shared:\n\n1. They've worked at a museum on a contract basis for 11 years\n2. They were told their contract might not be renewed - could end in 3 months\n3. But what scares them more than losing the job itself is the prospect of going all day without talking to anyone\n\nThis is a rich disclosure. The client has:\n- A practical concern (job loss after 11 years)\n- A deeper emotional fear (social isolation, loss of connection)\n- They've prioritized what truly scares them (isolation over job loss)\n\nWhat would be a good counselor response here?\n\nI should:\n- Acknowledge both layers (the job and the deeper fear)\n- Honor that they've already identified what scares them most - the isolation\n- Not jump to solutions or reassurance\n- Not ask too many questions\n- Reflect the significance of what they've said - 11 years means this workplace was likely a major source of social connection\n- Maybe gently explore or reflect on the isolation fear without being pushy\n\nI want to avoid:\n- \"It'll be okay\" type reassurance\n- Jumping to \"have you thought about other jobs?\"\n- Overly clinical language\n- Asking multiple questions\n- Ignoring the emotional depth in favor of the practical issue\n\nI think a good response would acknowledge the 11 years, validate that the fear of isolation makes complete sense given how much of their daily social life was built into that workplace, and perhaps gently open the door to exploring that isolation fear further - without forcing it.\n\nLet me write something natural and warm in Korean.\n",
